/* data/learn-c/phy-11-07.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-07.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-07"] = {"8.1":{"s":"A wave carries energy and information from one place to another without carrying matter along with it. Sound, light, water waves and waves on a string are common examples. This chapter covers common properties of waves, transverse and longitudinal waves, the mathematical form of a travelling wave, the speed of sound, echoes, the qualities of sound and the Doppler effect.","w":["A wave moves energy, not matter. The particles of the medium only oscillate about their places.","Sound needs a medium; light does not."],"x":["A wave is a disturbance that moves through a medium, or through space, and carries energy and information from one place to another. The matter of the medium is not carried along. Sound, light, water ripples and waves on a string are common examples.","This chapter first lists the properties that all waves share. Then it separates transverse and longitudinal waves, writes the equation of a travelling wave, and gives the speed of waves on a string and of sound in a gas. After that, it studies how two waves add up (superposition), echo and reverberation, the qualities of sound, and the Doppler effect.","Study tip: learn the three relations that are used in nearly every problem: v = nλ, v = √(T/m) for a string, and the Doppler formula with the right signs."],"e":"A cork floats on water, and a ripple passes it. The cork goes up and down about its place. It is not carried away with the ripple, but the energy of the ripple travels on."},"8.2":{"s":"All waves share some properties. They transfer energy through a medium by the vibration of its particles, and the particles themselves only oscillate about their mean positions. Waves are described by amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period and speed. They can reflect, refract, interfere and diffract. Mechanical waves need a medium, while electromagnetic waves do not and can travel in vacuum.","w":["Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength. When the medium changes, frequency stays the same but speed and wavelength change.","Do not confuse the speed of the wave with the speed of the vibrating particle."],"x":["All waves share some properties. They carry energy from one place to another through the vibration of the particles of a medium, and the particles only oscillate about their mean positions. A wave is described by its amplitude a (the greatest displacement), its wavelength λ (the distance between two successive crests), its frequency n (the number of waves per second), and its period T = 1/n.","The speed of a wave is v = nλ. The speed is fixed by the medium. If a wave goes from one medium to another, the frequency does not change, since it is set by the source. The speed and the wavelength change in the same ratio.","Waves can be reflected, refracted, and they can interfere and diffract. These properties apply to sound, light and water waves. The intensity of a wave is the energy that passes through a unit area in one second, and it is proportional to the square of the amplitude.","Common mistake: thinking that the speed of a wave depends on its frequency in a given medium. For sound in air, all frequencies travel at the same speed."],"e":"A note of 250 Hz in air has a wavelength of 340/250 = 1.36 m. In water, where the speed is about 1500 m/s, the same note has a wavelength of 6 m, but still a frequency of 250 Hz."},"8.3":{"s":"In a transverse wave the particles of the medium vibrate at right angles to the direction in which the wave travels; it moves as crests and troughs. In a longitudinal wave the particles vibrate along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions. Sound in air is longitudinal. Transverse waves need a rigid medium, while longitudinal waves travel in solids, liquids and gases. Only transverse waves can be polarised.","f":[{"x":"Solid: v = √(Y/ρ) ; liquid: v = √(B/ρ)","n":"Speed of a longitudinal wave. Y is Young's modulus, B is bulk modulus."},{"x":"v = √(T/μ)","n":"Speed of a transverse wave on a string. T is the tension, μ is mass per unit length."}],"w":["Sound is longitudinal, so it cannot be polarised."],"x":["In a transverse wave, the particles of the medium vibrate at right angles to the direction in which the wave travels. The wave moves as crests and troughs. Examples: waves on a string, light and other electromagnetic waves. Transverse waves need a medium that can resist a shear, so in the bulk of a liquid or a gas they cannot travel, but they can in a solid.","In a longitudinal wave, the particles vibrate along the direction in which the wave travels. The wave moves as compressions, where the particles are close and the pressure is high, and rarefactions, where they are far apart and the pressure is low. Sound in air is the standard example. Longitudinal waves can travel in solids, liquids and gases.","A transverse wave can be polarised, because the vibration is in a plane at right angles to the direction of the wave. A longitudinal wave cannot be polarised. This is why light can be polarised, and sound cannot.","Common mistake: thinking that sound waves in air have crests and troughs of the air. They are compressions and rarefactions, and the particles move back and forth along the line of travel."],"e":"A slinky spring is stretched. A sideways flick sends a transverse wave, and a push along its length sends a longitudinal wave. Both move along the spring, with different kinds of motion of its coils."},"8.4":{"s":"A sinusoidal travelling wave is written as a function of position and time. The amplitude a is the greatest displacement, ω the angular frequency, k the angular wave number, and the whole bracket is the phase. A minus sign between kx and ωt means the wave moves towards positive x; a plus sign means it moves towards negative x. Each particle performs simple harmonic motion with the same amplitude and frequency, but a different phase.","f":[{"x":"y = a*sin(k*x - omega*t + phi)","n":"Wave travelling in the +x direction: a = amplitude, k = angular wave number, omega = angular frequency, phi = initial phase.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.4, p.145"},{"x":"y = a*sin(k*x + omega*t + phi)","n":"Wave travelling in the -x direction.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.4, p.145"}],"w":["The sign between kx and ωt tells the direction: minus for +x, plus for −x."],"x":["A sinusoidal travelling wave is written as y = a sin(kx − ωt + φ). Here y is the displacement, a is the amplitude, k = 2π/λ is the angular wave number (rad/m), ω = 2πn = 2π/T is the angular frequency (rad/s), and the whole bracket, kx − ωt + φ, is the phase. The phase at the start (x = 0, t = 0) is φ.","The sign between kx and ωt gives the direction. For y = a sin(kx − ωt), the wave goes in the +x direction, since a point of constant phase has x = (ωt + const)/k, which increases. For y = a sin(kx + ωt), it goes in the −x direction. The speed is v = ω/k = nλ.","To read off a wave from its equation, compare it with the standard form. For y = 0.02 sin(3x − 900t), a = 0.02 m, k = 3 rad/m, ω = 900 rad/s, so λ = 2π/3 = 2.09 m, n = 900/2π = 143 Hz, and v = 300 m/s, in the +x direction.","Common mistake: finding the speed as k/ω, which is upside down. The speed is ω/k."],"e":"For y = 5 sin(2πx − 400πt) in centimetres, a = 5 cm, k = 2π so λ = 1 cm, ω = 400π so n = 200 Hz, and v = ω/k = 200 cm/s. It moves towards +x."},"8.5":{"s":"The speed of a wave depends on the medium. On a stretched string it is √(T/m), with tension T and mass per unit length m. In a fluid or solid, longitudinal waves travel at √(E/ρ), with E the modulus of elasticity and ρ the density. Newton assumed an isothermal process and got too low a value; Laplace showed it is adiabatic, which adds γ. Speed in air rises with temperature and humidity, not with pressure.","f":[{"x":"v = sqrt(T/m)","n":"Speed of transverse waves on a stretched string: T = tension, m = mass per unit length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.5, p.146"},{"x":"v = sqrt(E/rho)","n":"Speed of longitudinal waves: E = modulus of elasticity of the medium, rho = density.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.5, p.146"},{"x":"v = sqrt(gamma*P/rho)","n":"Laplace's formula for the speed of sound in a gas: gamma = C_p/C_v (1.41 for air), P = pressure, rho = density.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.5, p.147"},{"x":"v/v_0 = sqrt(T/T_0)","n":"Speed of sound at kelvin temperatures T and T_0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.5, p.148"},{"x":"v_t = v_0 + 0.61*t","n":"Speed of sound in air at t degrees Celsius (v_0 = 332 m/s at 0 degrees Celsius).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.5, p.149"}],"w":["Changing the pressure of a gas at constant temperature does not change the speed of sound.","Sound travels faster in moist air than in dry air, because moist air is less dense.","The speed in air rises about 0.61 m/s for every degree Celsius."],"x":["The speed of a wave depends on the medium, and not on the source. For a transverse wave on a stretched string, v = √(T/m), where T is the tension and m is the mass per unit length. A higher tension gives a faster wave, and a heavier string gives a slower one. If T becomes four times, v becomes twice.","For longitudinal waves in a medium, v = √(E/ρ), where E is the modulus of elasticity and ρ is the density. For a solid rod, E is Young's modulus, and for a fluid, it is the bulk modulus. In a gas, Newton thought that the compressions are slow, so the temperature stays constant, which gives v = √(P/ρ), and this value is too small. Laplace corrected it by saying that the changes are so quick that no heat flows. The change is adiabatic, so v = √(γP/ρ) = √(γRT/M). For air at 0 °C, it is 332 m/s.","Factors: the speed in a gas does not depend on the pressure if the temperature is constant, since P/ρ stays the same. It is proportional to √T, and rises by about 0.61 m/s for each degree Celsius: v_t = 331 + 0.61t. It is larger in moist air, which is less dense. It is much larger in solids and liquids than in gases.","Common mistake: thinking that the speed of sound is higher at a higher pressure for a gas at the same temperature. It stays the same."],"e":"A string of 0.040 kg/m with a tension of 100 N: v = √(100/0.040) = 50 m/s. Sound in air at 25 °C: v = 331 + 0.61 × 25 = 346 m/s."},"8.6":{"s":"Principle of superposition: when two or more waves overlap in a medium, the resultant displacement at each point is the vector sum of the displacements that each wave would produce on its own. After they meet, the waves carry on unchanged, as if they had not met. This explains interference of waves and the formation of beats and stationary waves, which are studied in Std XII.","f":[{"x":"y = y₁ + y₂","n":"Principle of superposition. Displacements add at every point."},{"x":"A = √(A₁² + A₂² + 2A₁A₂ cos φ)","n":"Resultant amplitude of two waves. φ is the phase difference."}],"w":["Superposition adds displacements, not intensities. Add with signs, then square for intensity.","After overlapping, the waves move on unchanged; they do not destroy each other."],"x":["The principle of superposition: when two or more waves overlap in a medium, the resultant displacement at each point is the vector sum of the displacements that each wave would produce alone. After they meet, the waves continue on their way, unchanged, as if the others had not been there.","For two waves of the same frequency with amplitudes a and b, travelling in the same direction with a phase difference φ, the resultant has the amplitude A = √(a² + b² + 2ab cos φ). In phase (φ = 0), A = a + b, the largest value, which is constructive interference. In opposite phase (φ = 180°), A = |a − b|, the smallest value, which is destructive interference. The intensity is proportional to A².","Superposition explains the beats, the stationary waves on a string, and the patterns of interference in light and sound. For a stable pattern, the waves must have the same frequency and a fixed phase difference, which means that the sources are coherent.","Common mistake: adding the intensities in place of the amplitudes. First add the displacements, and then square to get the intensity."],"e":"Waves of 3 cm and 4 cm amplitude: in phase, the amplitude is 7 cm. In opposite phase, it is 1 cm. With a phase difference of 90°, it is √(9 + 16) = 5 cm. At 60°, it is √37 = 6.08 cm."},"8.7":{"s":"An echo is sound that is reflected from a distant, hard surface and heard again. The ear keeps a sound for about 0.1 s, so to hear a distinct echo the reflector must be at least about 17 m away. Reverberation is the persistence of sound in a hall because of repeated reflections. Acoustics is the design of rooms so that sound is heard clearly, avoiding too much echo or reverberation.","f":[{"x":"d = vt/2","n":"Echo. Minimum distance for a distinct echo is about 17 m (0.1 s persistence)."},{"x":"T = 0.161 V / A","n":"Sabine's formula for reverberation time. V is the room volume in m³, A the total absorption in m² (sabine)."}],"w":["The sound travels to the wall AND back, so the wall must be half the distance the sound covers in 0.1 s."],"x":["An echo is a sound that is reflected from a distant hard surface, such as a cliff or a large wall, and heard again after the original sound. The ear keeps the impression of a sound for about 0.1 s. So, for a clear echo, the reflected sound must arrive at least 0.1 s later. The sound goes to the surface and back, so the distance travelled is twice the distance to the surface. At 340 m/s, in 0.1 s the sound covers 34 m, so the surface must be at least 17 m away.","The depth of the sea, or the distance to an object, can be found from an echo: d = vt/2. This is the idea of the sonar and of the use of ultrasound in medicine.","Reverberation is the persistence of sound in a hall after the source has stopped, because of repeated reflections from the walls, the floor and the roof. A small amount makes the sound richer, but too much makes speech unclear. Acoustics is the design of a hall to control it, with absorbing materials such as curtains, carpets, soft seats and special wall panels.","Common mistake: using the distance to the wall as the full distance the sound travels. The sound travels twice as far."],"e":"A sonar pulse returns from the sea bottom after 0.80 s. The speed of sound in sea water is 1500 m/s. The depth is d = 1500 × 0.80/2 = 600 m."},"8.8":{"s":"Sound has three qualities. Pitch depends on frequency: a higher frequency is a shriller note. Quality (timbre) depends on the overtones mixed with the fundamental and lets us tell two instruments apart. Loudness depends on the intensity and is measured on a logarithmic scale in decibels. The threshold of hearing is 10⁻¹² W/m². An increase of 10 dB means ten times the intensity.","f":[{"x":"L_dB = 10*log_10(I/I_0)","n":"Loudness in decibels: I = intensity, I_0 = 10^-12 W/m^2, the least audible intensity.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.8, p.152"},{"x":"L_bel = log_10(I/I_0)","n":"Loudness in bel (1 bel = 10 decibel).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.8, p.152"}],"w":["Every 10 dB increase is a TEN-fold increase in intensity, not a doubling.","Loudness is not the same as intensity: it depends on how the ear responds."],"x":["A musical sound has three qualities. Pitch depends on the frequency. A higher frequency gives a shriller note, such as that of a female voice. Quality, or timbre, depends on the overtones that are mixed with the fundamental note, and it lets us tell two instruments apart, though they play the same note at the same loudness. Loudness depends on the intensity, and also on how the ear responds.","The intensity of a sound is the energy that passes in one second through a unit area, in W/m². The ear can hear from about 10⁻¹² W/m² (the threshold, I₀) to 1 W/m² (the threshold of pain). So the sound level is measured on a logarithmic scale: L = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀) decibels (dB), or log₁₀(I/I₀) bel.","Each rise of 10 dB is a factor of 10 in the intensity, and 20 dB is a factor of 100. It is not a doubling. A whisper is about 30 dB, normal speech about 60 dB, and a jet engine at a short distance about 120 dB. A sound that is above about 85 dB for a long time can damage hearing.","Common mistake: thinking that loudness and intensity are the same. Another mistake: adding decibels directly. Two equal sources raise the level by only 3 dB."],"e":"A sound of 10⁻⁶ W/m² has a level of 10 log₁₀(10⁶) = 60 dB. If the distance from a point source is tripled, the intensity falls to 1/9, and the level falls by 9.5 dB."},"8.9":{"s":"The Doppler effect is the change in the frequency heard when the source of sound and the listener move relative to each other. The frequency is higher when they approach and lower when they move apart. It depends only on the relative motion along the line joining them. The speed of sound in the medium stays fixed. The effect also occurs for light.","f":[{"x":"n = n_0*v/(v + v_s)","n":"Source moving away from a stationary listener at speed v_s: n_0 = true frequency, v = speed of sound. For a source moving towards the listener use v - v_s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.9, p.154"},{"x":"n = n_0*(v + v_L)/v","n":"Listener approaching a stationary source at speed v_L; use v - v_L when the listener moves away.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.9, p.155"},{"x":"n = n_0*(v +- v_L)/(v -+ v_s)","n":"General case, both moving: choose the upper signs when they approach each other and the lower signs when they move apart.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.9, p.155"},{"x":"delta n/n = v_r/v","n":"Small relative speed v_r (much less than v): fractional change of frequency.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 8.9, p.155"}],"w":["Approach means HIGHER frequency, recede means LOWER; the speed of sound itself never changes.","Take the direction from the listener to the source as positive when you decide the signs."],"x":["The Doppler effect is the change in the frequency of a sound that is heard when the source and the listener are in relative motion. The frequency is higher when they approach each other, and lower when they move apart. The pitch of the horn of a fast train rises as it comes towards you and falls after it passes.","Why it happens: a moving source follows its own waves, so in front of it the crests are closer together and the wavelength is shorter, and behind it they are further apart. A moving listener meets more crests, or fewer crests, each second. The speed of sound in the air itself does not change.","The formulas, with v the speed of sound: a source moving with the speed v_s: n = n₀v/(v ∓ v_s), with the minus sign for approach and the plus sign for recession. A listener moving with the speed v_L: n = n₀(v ± v_L)/v, with the plus sign for approach. For both moving: n = n₀(v ± v_L)/(v ∓ v_s). For a small relative speed v_r, the change is Δn/n = v_r/v.","A good rule: take the direction from the listener towards the source as positive, and write the velocities with signs. Common mistake: giving the wrong sign to the speeds, or thinking that the speed of the sound wave changes."],"e":"A source of 500 Hz moves at 20 m/s towards a listener who is at rest, with v = 340 m/s: n = 500 × 340/320 = 531 Hz. If the listener also moves towards the source at 10 m/s: n = 500 × 350/320 = 547 Hz."}};
